the bacterium&#x27;s own array have no PAM beside them, so Cas9 does not cut its own record.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Search and cut&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that Cas9 can be programmed: fuse the two RNAs into a single guide, write the sequence you want into it, and Cas9 cuts DNA wherever that sequence occurs. The cell then repairs the break, and the repair is where editing happens: the gene can be switched off, or, given a template, rewritten.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A tool for any DNA&quot;}]&lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;figure&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;importmap&quot;&gt;{&quot;imports&quot;: {&quot;three&quot;: &quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;raskell.io&#x2F;js&#x2F;vendor&#x2F;three&#x2F;three.module.min.js&quot;}}&lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;raskell.io&#x2F;js&#x2F;crispr-3d.js?h=b401240eac6bee6e5e52&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The history is the best part of the chapter. In 1987 a Japanese doctoral student, Yoshizumi Ishino, noticed an odd pattern while sequencing a gene in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;: short identical stretches of DNA repeated at regular intervals, with different stretches in between. He mentioned it in the last lines of his paper, said nobody knew what it was for, and moved on. A few years later Francisco Mojica, a graduate student in Alicante studying microbes that live in salt ponds, found the same pattern, first assumed he had made a mistake, and then spent a decade trying to explain it with little funding and much advice to stop.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The answer came in August 2003, when he ran the spacers through a database and found that they matched the DNA of viruses that attack those bacteria. Bacteria, it turned out, have an immune system that remembers. Journals were slow to believe it; the paper took until 2005 to appear. Two food scientists at the Danish company Danisco, Rodolphe Barrangou and Philippe Horvath, confirmed it in their collection of yogurt bacteria: strains collected after a virus attack carried new spacers that matched that virus. And in 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed how the cutting works and that it can be programmed, which turned a bacterial defence into a tool for editing any DNA.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What I find most elegant is how little it takes. A library of short snippets, a way to copy them into guides, and one enzyme that compares a guide with whatever DNA it meets. The same pattern of matching a stored sample against what arrives is how a lot of security systems work, and it is a fine example of curiosity paying off: a question about salt-loving microbes that turned, years later, into one of the most useful tools in biology.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
